lord’s 9s, possibly because they didn’t want to waste a high pair or because they are saving cards. That implies they may prefer that *I* take this trick instead.\n\nStill, playing the Aces now risks wasting them early. The Jacks (♠J♣J) are sufficient to beat 9s and are safer than Aces. 10s are even lower, but Jacks are better because they leave 10s for potential future use in sequences or as fillers.\n\nHowever, consider signaling: by playing a mid-level pair like Jacks, I signal strength without overcommitting. It allows me to clear a pair while keeping both 10s and Aces for later coordination.\n\nBut wait — do I want to take control at all? If I pass, the teammate must respond. But they already passed, indicating they don’t want to play here. So if I pass, the trick ends with the landlord’s 9s, and they continue leading — which gives them tempo advantage. That’s bad.\n\nTherefore, **someone must beat the 9s**, and since the teammate passed, it falls to me. I should beat it, but with the minimal necessary power.\n\nBetween the options:\n- ♠10♦10: beats 9s, safe\n- ♠J♣J: stronger, also safe\n- ♠A♦A: strongest, overkill\n\nBest choice: **♠J♣J** — it\'s higher than needed, but not excessively so. It preserves Aces and 10s (which could form part of a sequence or future pair plays). Also, I have no use for the Js otherwise — no sequence around them, and K-Q-J would require Qs I don’t have.\n\nReason: I must beat the landlord’s pair to avoid giving them free leads. Passing would let them maintain initiative. Among the valid pairs, ♠J♣J is sufficient to win without overusing high cards like Aces. It conserves more valuable cards (Aces, 10s) for later coordination with my teammate and avoids premature depletion of top-tier pairs.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠J", "♣J"]}'}
